Cell[TextData[{
 "Figure 2.1  Monthly occurrence density of the Arctic tern from the GBIF / \
eBird Observation Dataset (2014\[Dash]2024), one panel per calendar month, \
750-km hex bins, Robinson projection. The northward breeding-season \
concentration (May\[Dash]July) and the long southbound Atlantic passage are \
both visible. ",
 StyleBox["But read the next caption before trusting any apparent geography.",
  
  FontSlant->Italic]
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{70, 70}, {2, 14}},
 FontSize->11,
 FontSlant->Italic,
 FontColor->GrayLevel[
  0.35],ExpressionUUID->"cb669041-ccb8-4084-91e5-d8e89e2c4586"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["The honest caveat: effort bias.",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " Occurrence records do not report where the birds are \[LongDash] they \
report where the ",
 StyleBox["observers",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " are. eBird coverage is overwhelmingly concentrated in North America and \
Western Europe, where most contributors live, and is sparse across the open \
ocean, the tropics, and the Southern Hemisphere. A naive density map \
therefore conflates \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]many birds here\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] with \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]many birders here.\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] Any honest analysis of citizen-science occurrence \
data must correct for this sampling bias before drawing geographic \
conclusions \[LongDash] which is exactly what the centroid method in \
\[Section]3 is built to do."
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"7177fed2-d81a-450e-ad4d-950fbd02185c"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["3.  From checklists to centroids (method)", "Section",
 FontFamily->"Roboto Condensed",
 FontSize->30,
 FontColor->RGBColor[
  0.153, 0.51, 0.64],ExpressionUUID->"b177a247-d4bd-40d0-9a5f-c33d2c02a1de"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["The idea.",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " To summarise \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]where the population is\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] in a given month with a single point, we compute the \
",
 StyleBox["monthly population centroid",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " \[LongDash] a weighted average location of that month's occurrences. Done \
naively this inherits all the effort bias of \[Section]2 and is geometrically \
wrong for a round planet. Two corrections fix both problems."
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"1bbd2b1a-3f1a-4949-b8ff-1aefcdf61c93"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["3.1  Correction one: presence weighting on a coarse grid", "Subsection",
 FontFamily->"Roboto Condensed",
 FontColor->RGBColor[
  0.153, 0.51, 0.64],ExpressionUUID->"956b0c74-b1a6-4e62-bff2-84de5af8d261"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "A raw average over occurrence ",
 StyleBox["points",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " is dominated by a handful of intensely-birded hotspots \[LongDash] a \
single famous reserve can contribute tens of thousands of records and drag \
the centroid toward itself. To blunt this, we lay down a coarse ",
 StyleBox["2\[Degree] grid",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " and weight each ",
 StyleBox["occupied cell",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " equally (presence = 1 per cell), rather than weighting by the number of \
records in the cell. A cell with 40,000 checklists and a cell with one both \
count once. This converts \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]where were people looking\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] into \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]where was the species \
present,\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] which is far closer to the quantity we \
want."
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"a569b27e-ea51-4b99-a082-e3bccdbfc362"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["A subtlety for the specialist.",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " A 2\[Degree]\[Times]2\[Degree] cell is ",
 StyleBox["not",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " equal-area: its true area shrinks toward the poles in proportion to ",
 StyleBox["cos(lat)",
  FontFamily->"Times",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 ". For high-latitude species this matters, and the spherical mean below \
handles it correctly by construction \[LongDash] each presence point is \
converted to a unit vector whose contribution is naturally weighted by the \
geometry of the sphere, not by a flat lon\[Times]lat rectangle."
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"a4718977-1124-49f5-a62e-633ae02ac17d"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["3.2  Correction two: a proper spherical centroid", "Subsection",
 FontFamily->"Roboto Condensed",
 FontColor->RGBColor[
  0.153, 0.51, 0.64],ExpressionUUID->"da7c71d1-1212-48e4-9a50-5042d02f93b9"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The deeper problem is that ",
 StyleBox["you cannot average longitudes arithmetically",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 ". Longitude wraps at \[PlusMinus]180\[Degree]: the mean of +170\[Degree] \
and \[Minus]170\[Degree] is not 0\[Degree] (the Atlantic) \[LongDash] it \
should be 180\[Degree] (the dateline). For a circumpolar bird like the Arctic \
tern, whose occurrences ring the entire planet, a naive longitude average is \
simply meaningless. The correct construction embeds each point on the unit \
sphere, averages the 3-D vectors, and projects back:"
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"a1218b18-0a0d-4e14-a59f-10e9d17fef0b"],

Cell["\<\
(* Weighted spherical mean of (lat,lon) points. Returns {lat,lon}
   in degrees, or Missing[\"DegenerateCentroid\"] when the resultant
   vector is ~0 (antipodally smeared points cancel) -- we never fabricate
   an arithmetic-mean longitude. (This is the helper in the package.) *)
sphericalCentroid[lats_, lons_, w_] := Module[{v, m},
  v = MapThread[Function[{la, lo, wt},
        wt {Cos[la Degree] Cos[lo Degree],
            Cos[la Degree] Sin[lo Degree],
            Sin[la Degree]}], {lats, lons, w}];
  m = Total[v];
  If[Norm[m] < 10^-9, Return[Missing[\"DegenerateCentroid\"]]];
  m = m/Norm[m];
  {ArcSin[m[[3]]]/Degree, ArcTan[m[[1]], m[[2]]]/Degree}];\
\>", "Input",
 CellMargins->{{60, 30}, {6, 6}},
 FontSize->11,ExpressionUUID->"03a17d33-0f92-4cef-9479-6ee61248b427"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The ",
 StyleBox["Missing[\"DegenerateCentroid\"]",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 " branch is not defensive boilerplate \[LongDash] it is the honest answer \
when a population is spread so evenly around the globe (or split into \
antipodal clumps) that ",
 StyleBox["no",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " single point represents it. Returning a fabricated longitude there would \
be worse than returning nothing. As we will see in \[Section]4, this is \
precisely the situation a global centroid walks into for a multi-flyway \
species, and the reason we must split by flyway first."
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"5a9fd875-57df-4779-a5e6-da9d08b9144c"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["4.  The multi-flyway trap (the key lesson)", "Section",
 FontFamily->"Roboto Condensed",
 FontSize->30,
 FontColor->RGBColor[
  0.153, 0.51, 0.64],ExpressionUUID->"630cb069-88bc-44f5-8ac7-1953bb9182ab"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["The trap.",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " A spherical centroid is only meaningful if the points it averages belong \
to a single, ",
 StyleBox["coherent",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " population. Many species do not: they split into geographically disjoint \
",
 StyleBox["flyways",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " that never mix. Averaging across flyways produces a \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]centre\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] that lies where ",
 StyleBox["no birds are",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " \[LongDash] the midpoint of two crowds is the empty space between them."
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"5d42d777-a0b2-4134-b66d-8d9cb89ceadc"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The red knot is the textbook case. Pool its Americas, European/African, and \
Asian-Australasian flyways into one global centroid and the result drifts out \
into the open ",
 StyleBox["mid-Atlantic",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 ", on average ",
 StyleBox["~4,670 km per month",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " away from where the Americas (",
 StyleBox["rufa",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 ") population \[LongDash] the one a North American reader actually sees \
\[LongDash] truly is. The number is not a rounding artefact; it is the \
signature of averaging across populations that should never have been \
combined."
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"3e3e2192-acae-4b1c-b84f-02c560c31dc8"],

Cell["\<\
(* The fix: restrict to one coherent flyway BEFORE taking the
   centroid. Each box is {lon -> {min,max}, lat -> {min,max}}. *)
swift = monthlyCentroids[5228676, <|\"lon\" -> {-30, 145},  \"lat\" -> {-40, \
75}|>];  (* Afro-Palearctic *)
knot  = monthlyCentroids[2481765, <|\"lon\" -> {-110, -30}, \"lat\" -> {-56, \
85}|>];  (* Americas flyway *)
tern  = monthlyCentroids[5229230, <|\"lon\" -> {-80, 20},   \"lat\" -> {-90, \
90}|>];  (* Atlantic basin *)

(* The BROKEN contrast: no flyway filter -> meaningless mid-ocean centroid *)
knotGlobal = monthlyCentroids[2481765, All];\
\>", "Input",
 CellMargins->{{60, 30}, {6, 6}},
 FontSize->11,ExpressionUUID->"41ae1fa2-ecf4-4d80-b327-f3c243eb2d81"],

Cell["\<\
With each species reduced to a single coherent flyway, the monthly centroids \
trace clean, interpretable migration loops. The common swift's \
Afro-Palearctic flyway needs no special handling \[LongDash] it is already \
one population, and its centroid simply works. The red knot is shown on its \
Americas flyway. The Arctic tern is shown as an Atlantic-basin centroid (and, \
separately, as a latitude-versus-month curve, since its defining signal is \
the pole-to-pole north\[Dash]south sweep):\
\>", "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"9cbab13d-4588-4d9e-bf61-a6135b93a0a2"],

